1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,324
Good afternoon everyone.

2
00:00:01,324 --> 00:00:06,202
Well, with some of you about to go out on field work it's timely

3
00:00:06,202 --> 00:00:09,850
that in this afternoon's session I'll be sharing some ideas

4
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:16,614
about the reasons why groups of whales and dolphins sometimes swim ashore from the sea right onto the beach

5
00:00:16,614 --> 00:00:20,530
and, most often, die in what are known as 'mass strandings'.

6
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:25,997
Unfortunately, this type of event is a frequent occurrence in some of the locations

7
00:00:25,997 --> 00:00:27,950
that you'll be travelling to,

8
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:33,020
where sometimes the tide goes out suddenly, confusing the animals.

9
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:38,040
However, there are many other theories about the causes of mass strandings.

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:42,720
The first is that the behaviour is linked to parasites.

11
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:48,090
It's often found that stranded animals were infested with large numbers of parasites.

12
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:53,195
For instance, a type of worm is commonly found in the ears of dead

13
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:53,560
whales.

14
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:59,533
Since marine animals rely heavily on their hearing to navigate, this type of infestation

15
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:02,520
has the potential to be very harmful!

16
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:06,383
Another theory is related to toxins,

17
00:01:06,383 --> 00:01:07,670
or poisons.

18
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:12,690
These have also been found to contribute to the death of many marine animals.

19
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:17,948
Many toxins, as I'm sure you're aware, originate from plants,

20
00:01:17,948 --> 00:01:19,000
or animals.

21
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The whale ingests these toxins in its normal feeding behaviour

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,805
but whether these poisons directly or indirectly lead to stranding

23
00:01:27,805 --> 00:01:31,230
and death, seems to depend upon the toxin involved.

24
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:38,291
In 1988, for example, fourteen humpback whales examined after stranding along the beaches of

25
00:01:38,291 --> 00:01:39,300
Cape Cod

26
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:45,080
were found to have been poisoned after eating tuna that contained saxitoxin, the same

27
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,970
toxin that can be fatal in humans.

28
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:54,951
Alternatively, it has also been suggested that some animals strand accidentally by following their

29
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:58,940
prey ashore in the confusion of the chase.

30
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:06,470
In 1995 David Thurston monitored pilot whales that beached after following squid ashore.

31
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:11,846
However, this idea does not seem to hold true for the majority of mass

32
00:02:11,846 --> 00:02:12,230
strandings

33
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:17,525
because examination of the animals' stomach contents reveal that most had not been feeding

34
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:18,660
as they stranded.

35
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:24,060
There are also some new theories which link strandings to humans.

36
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:29,430
A growing concern is that loud noises in the ocean cause strandings.

37
00:02:29,430 --> 00:02:34,140
Noises such as those caused by military exercises are of particular concern

38
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:38,850
and have been pinpointed as the cause of some strandings of late.

39
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:45,065
One of these, a mass stranding of whales in 2000 in the Bahamas coincided

40
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:49,060
closely with experiments using a new submarine detection system.

41
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:54,604
There were several factors that made this stranding stand out as different from previous

42
00:02:54,604 --> 00:02:55,000
strandings.

43
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,939
This led researchers to look for a new cause.

44
00:02:58,939 --> 00:03:02,440
For one, all the stranded animals were healthy.

45
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:07,794
In addition, the animals were spread out along 38 kilometres of coast, whereas it's

46
00:03:07,794 --> 00:03:13,148
more common for the animals to be found in a group when mass strandings

47
00:03:13,148 --> 00:03:13,530
occur.

48
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:17,510
A final theory is related to group behaviour,

49
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:23,480
and suggests that sea mammals cannot distinguish between sick and healthy leaders

50
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:27,800
and will follow sick leaders, even to an inevitable death.

51
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:33,281
This is a particularly interesting theory since the whales that are thought to be

52
00:03:33,281 --> 00:03:38,370
most social - the toothed whales - are the group that strand the most frequently.

53
00:03:38,370 --> 00:03:44,840
The theory is also supported by evidence from a dolphin stranding in 1994.

54
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:49,606
Examination of the dead animals revealed that apart from the leader, all the others

55
00:03:49,606 --> 00:03:52,670
had been healthy at the time of their death.

56
00:03:52,670 --> 00:03:58,604
Without one consistent theory however it is very hard for us to do anything

57
00:03:58,604 --> 00:04:03,690
about this phenomenon except to assist animals where and when we can.

58
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:08,918
Stranding networks have been established around the world to aid in rescuing animals

59
00:04:08,918 --> 00:04:12,940
and collecting samples from those that could not be helped.

60
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:18,944
I recommend John Connor's Marine Mammals Ashore as an excellent starting point if you're

61
00:04:18,944 --> 00:04:22,375
interested in finding out more about these networks,

62
00:04:22,375 --> 00:04:24,090
or establishing one yourself.
